== Parents Admonishing and Correcting Children

=== §. 42. #emph[Of adding admonition to instruction.]

The means of furthering the good work of nurture #footnote[III. The means of nurture.] are chiefly two.

+ Frequent admonition.
+ Due correction.

Both of them are implied in this text: one in the word translated #emph[admonition,] which (according to the derivation of the #footnote[νουθεσία. See Treatise 1, §. 120.] Greek word) is a putting of a thing into the mind, an urging and pressing of it; the other in the word translated #emph[nurture.] #footnote[παιδεία. See Treatise 1, §. 119.]

Now both of these are to be joined together, as being very helpful to each other. For admonition without correction is likely to prove mere futility, and correction without admonition will be too much austerity.

The duty which the first of these sets forth is this:

#emph[Parents must often whet instruction upon their children.] They #footnote[#emph[Annuncia, praecipe, impera, suade eis, etc. Aug. de salut. docum. cap.] 29. \[Declare, enjoin, command, persuade them, etc.\]] must not think it enough to tell their children what they ought to do; to instruction they must add admonition, and, as it were, beat into their children’s heads the lessons which they teach them, so that these may make a deeper impression on their hearts. In this way their instructions will be like the “words of the wise,” which are “like nails firmly fastened,” or nails fast knocked #footnote[Eccl 12:11.] in. They remain firm where they are once fastened, and cannot easily be plucked out. For as many blows knock a nail in up to the head (as we say), so many admonitions settle good instructions in a child’s heart, and cause the heart to be established in what is taught, #footnote[Heb 13:9.] which is a thing to be labored after. The general exhortation to “be urgent in season and out of season”#footnote[2 Tim 4:2.] may be applied to this purpose; but more pertinently that #footnote[Deut 6:7. שנן #emph[Acuit. in Piel acuit valde, vel frequenter. Metaphora ducta à gladio, qui dum acuitur saepius impellitur ad cotem. Vatab. in annot. in hunc loc.] \[It sharpens; in Piel, it sharpens much, or often. A metaphor taken from a sword, which, while it is being sharpened, is driven again and again against the whetstone.\] #emph[vide] Treatise 1, §. 120.] direction which is given particularly to parents, of #emph[whetting] God’s words upon their children.

It is to this purpose that Solomon is accustomed to double his instructions and urge them again and again, as: “Listen … to your father’s instruction, and do not forsake the teaching”;#footnote[Prov 1:8.] “accept my words and hide my commandments within you; … incline your ear …, direct your heart,” etc.#footnote[Prov 2:1, 2. #emph[Direction.]] Indeed, he often repeats the very same precepts.

The understanding of children is fickle, and their memory weak. If they are instructed only once, or seldom, or carelessly, what is taught will soon slip away and do little or no good.

For the better performing of this duty, parents must think of the best means they can to fasten their instructions on their children, and observe their children’s inclination and disposition, and see by what they are most moved. Constant exhortations and powerful persuasions are included under admonition, and each in its kind is to be used as occasion requires.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of parents’ weariness in instructing their children.]

Contrary to this is the practice of those parents who soon grow weary of instructing their children. The apostle lays it down as a general caution in good duties that we “not grow #footnote[Gal 6:9.] weary.” If we must grow weary in no good thing, shall parents grow weary in doing good to their children? Yet how many there are who, having once taught their children, think they have done duty enough in that way: if their children will take it, they may. They are unwilling to take too much pains in often urging the points which they have taught them.

So that teaching vanishes away, and it turns out, as we say in the proverb, #emph[as good not to do it at all as to be never the better for it.] This is one point in which old Eli failed. For he gave his children very good instruction; but because he stopped there, it was neither accepted by God as a sufficient discharge of his duty, nor were his children any the better for it. If this was a fault in him, even though his children had come to years of discretion and to ripeness of understanding, how much greater is the fault in those whose children are still young?

#emph[Objection.] If a child does not take instruction at first, he is plainly of an unruly and perverse disposition; all the pains that can be taken will be lost.

#emph[Answer.] It may be childishness rather than perverseness, or some weakness rather than obstinacy. Considering the necessity of good nurture, no pains should be thought too much. There is more perverseness and unruliness in such parents as grow weary of doing this duty than in such children as are not moved at first; for this is a means ordained by God to cure this unruliness.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of parents reproving their children.]

The other means of helping nurture is correction.

This is of two sorts: verbal, by words, and real, by blows.

The former is #emph[reproof,] and it must always go before the latter, which is most usually and properly called #emph[correction.]

Reproof is a kind of middle thing between admonition and correction: it is a sharp admonition, but a mild correction.

It is all the more to be used because it may be a means of preventing strokes and blows, especially with well-disposed and good-natured children (for “a rebuke cuts into a man of discernment deeper than a hundred lashes cut into a fool”),#footnote[Prov 17:10.] and because it may be used when it is not so fitting to use strokes and blows, as when children have grown to full age.

The many good fruits which the Holy Spirit notes as coming from due reproof show that it is a duty which parents ought to make conscience of, as they desire to promote the good of their children; and all the more because many good fruits come back to the parents who reprove, as well as to the children reproved. As for the good of those who are reproved, it is said, “The reproofs of discipline are the way to life”;#footnote[Prov 6:23.] they bring “understanding”#footnote[Prov 15:32.] and make men “prudent.”#footnote[Prov 15:5.] As for the good of those who reprove, it is said, “To those who rebuke there will be delight”#footnote[Prov 24:25.] (that is, much comfort and cause for rejoicing, so that they will not need to regret what they have done) “and a blessing of good will come upon them”; that is, either a blessing of good men, who will bless, praise and commend them, or a blessing of good things, and that from the Lord, who will reward them for this conscientious performance of their duty.

On these grounds #footnote[Gen 9:25; 34:30; 49:4.] holy men have not held back from rebuking their children as there was occasion. Though Eli#footnote[1 Sam 2:23. #emph[Eli dum filijs molestus esse seuera increpatione recusat, & illos & seipsum una perdidit, Chrys. in] 1 #emph[Tim. hom.] 9. 1 Kgs 1:6.] did something in this duty, yet because he was not more severe in it, he brought destruction both on himself and on his children.

The direction noted in §. 40 and 47, and in Treatise 4, §. 35, may be applied here.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of parents pampering their children.]

Contrary to this is the excessive doting of parents on their children, when they are unwilling to give them a harsh word. David (though otherwise a very wise man) showed much folly in this, for he never once displeased Adonijah “by saying, ‘Why do you act this way?’” And it is very likely that he pampered his other rebellious son, Absalom, in the same way. Note the fearful outcome that followed from this, both for the father and for the children. Though their father would not displease them, yet they did not care about displeasing their father, indeed about grieving his heart and vexing his soul. All foolish doting parents may look for the same at their children’s hands. For, first, parents highly displease God by neglecting this duty; therefore in just retribution God will give their children over to #footnote[#emph[Omnia quae deliquerint filij, de parentibus requiruntur qui non erudierint filios suos. Orig. in Iob. lib.] 1.] displease and vex them. Secondly, neglect of reproof is a means of making children rude, presumptuous, rebellious, and so careless about pleasing their parents. Indeed, all things in which children offend through lack of upbringing will be required at their parents’ hands.

=== §. 46. #emph[Of correcting children.]

The latter and more proper kind of correction, which is by stripes and blows, is also a means appointed by God to help the good nurture and upbringing of children. It is the last remedy which a parent can use: a remedy which may do good when nothing else can.

The Holy Spirit both expressly commands it and very often presses it, in these and similar phrases: “Chasten your son”;#footnote[Prov 19:18.] “Correct your son”;#footnote[Prov 29:17.] “Do not withhold correction from a child”;#footnote[Prov 23:13.] “Strike him with a rod.”#footnote[#emph[v.] 14.] If there were no other motive, this would be sufficient. God’s charge was such a motive to Abraham that at it he would have sacrificed his son; #footnote[Gen 22:2, 3.] and will you not correct your child at God’s command?

It is further commended by God’s own example, which is set forth not only in some particular instances but in his general, constant dealing with all, and that as a special token and fruit of his love. For “the Lord chastens the one He loves, #footnote[Heb 12:6, 8.] and He chastises every son He receives. If you#super[+] do not experience chastening like everyone else, then you#super[+] are illegitimate children and not true sons.” Let this example of God be well weighed, for it is of great weight. Who can better tell what kind of dealing is fittest for children than God? Who can better nurture children than God? Who more truly aims at and secures the good of children than God? Indeed, who is more tender toward children than God? If God, #emph[the Father of spirits,] deals like this with his children in wisdom and love, #emph[fathers of the flesh] must not think to show wisdom or love by doing the contrary. Their wisdom will be folly, their love hatred. On these grounds it is taken as granted that parents (who care for the good of their children as they should) chastise their children as need requires. For it is said that the #footnote[Prov 3:12.] Lord corrects whom he loves, “as a father the son in whom he delights.” If parents did not use it, it would be no good inference to say, “as a father.” Again, it is said, as a thing beyond dispute, “we have had fathers of our flesh who corrected us.”#footnote[Heb 12:9.]

The grounds of the justice of this duty concern partly the #footnote[Reasons to move parents to correct.] children corrected, and partly the parents who correct. As for children, it frees them from much evil and works much good in them.

Correction is like medicine to purge out much corruption #footnote[1\. Benefits of correction to those corrected.] which lurks in children, and like a salve to heal many wounds and sores made by their folly. In this respect Solomon says that “foolishness is bound up in the heart of a child, but the rod of correction drives it far from him”;#footnote[Prov 22:15.] and again, “Lashes and wounds scour evil, and beatings cleanse the inmost parts.”#footnote[Prov 20:30.] Because of the inward working of this medicine, correction is further said to preserve a child from death#footnote[Prov 23:13.] (“if you strike him with a rod, he will not die”), and that not only from temporal death (as many children are preserved in this way from the magistrate’s sword) but also from eternal death#footnote[#emph[v.] 14.] (“you will deliver his soul from hell”). Note this, you#super[+] pampering parents, whose excessive leniency is very great cruelty. For may we not justly count him a cruel parent who would allow diseases, boils, sores and wounds to remain, increase and fester in his child, and give him no medicine, nor apply any plasters or remedies to him? Or rather, one who sees his son running into a flaming fire or deep water and would not hold him back? Just as cruel, and more cruel, are those who allow their children to run on in evil rather than correct them.

#emph[Objection.] Who can bear to make his own child smart and to put him to pain?

#emph[Answer.] The future fruit is to be considered more than the present pain. Potions, pills and corrosives are nauseating, bitter and painful; but because there is a necessity of using them, and great injury is prevented by their use, wise parents will not refrain from them because of the bitterness and pain that is felt. The apostle fitly answers that objection in this way: “No chastening #footnote[Heb 12:11.] seems enjoyable at the time, but painful. Later on, however, it yields a harvest of righteousness and peace.” This may be applied to parents’ corrections as well as to God’s.

The good which correction brings to children is noted by Solomon in this and similar phrases: “A rod of correction imparts wisdom.” #footnote[Prov 29:15.] For it makes children observe what is good and what evil, what is commendable and what blameworthy, and accordingly to do the good and leave the evil, which is a great point of wisdom.

#emph[Objection.] Instruction will do this better.

#emph[Answer.] Instruction may give them more knowledge, but it is correction which brings them to practice what they know, which is the #footnote[Matt 7:24.] chief point of wisdom.

As for parents, due correction of their children both frees them from many harms and also brings them #footnote[2\. Benefits of correcting to the one who corrects.] much quiet.

1\. It spares them much trouble. For many admonitions, often repeated and driven home again and again, will not make many children heed wholesome and good advice as much as a little correction will. They are much more sensitive to a smart than to words.

2\. It prevents much grief, shame and vexation. For “a foolish son brings grief to his father and bitterness to her who bore him.”#footnote[Prov 17:25.] But it is #footnote[Prov 22:15.] “the rod of correction” that drives foolishness away, and so prevents that #emph[grief] and #emph[bitterness.]

3\. It frees them from the guilt of their children’s sin, so that they are not accessories to it, as Eli#footnote[1 Sam 3:13.] was. For correction is the last remedy that a parent can use. If he can do no good by that, it is presumed that he has done his utmost; and so, though the child dies in his sin, yet the parent has delivered his own soul.

The quiet which is brought to parents by correcting their children is shown by Solomon in this way: “Correct your son, and he will give you rest; he will give delight to your soul.”#footnote[Prov 29:17.] For children well nurtured, and kept in a filial awe by correction, will conduct themselves so that their parents may rest fairly at ease and not trouble themselves (as they do with children left to their liberty). Indeed, like trees well pruned and ground well tilled, they will bring forth pleasant and abundant fruit; and so their parents will have just cause to rejoice in them.

=== §. 47. #emph[A direction to parents in correcting their children.]

To use this biting corrosive of correction well, parents must have regard to the #emph[matter] for which they correct, and to their #emph[manner] of correcting.

With respect to the matter, these three things must be noted. #footnote[Three things to be noted in the matter for which correction is to be given.]

1\. That they are sure a fault has been committed, so that there is just cause for correcting; otherwise more harm than good will come of it. If a corrosive is laid where there is no sore, it will make one. If correction is unjustly given, it may provoke to wrath, but will do little good. This is the point in which earthly fathers are blamed and shown to be unlike God,#footnote[Heb 12:10.] because many times they correct “at their own pleasure,” which is a point of great injustice.

2\. That the fault is made known to the child corrected, and he is clearly convicted of it. Correction must be for instruction, which it cannot be unless the child knows why he is corrected; for if he does not know his fault, it is all one to him as if he were corrected for no fault. God proceeded like this at the first with the serpent, #footnote[Gen 3:11, #emph[etc.]] with Eve, and with Adam. So judges proceed in punishing wrongdoers. Indeed, so men will deal with a dog. Should they not much more do so with a child?

3\. That the faults are especially such as the parents can show their children (at least if the children are of enough discretion) to be against God’s word, such as swearing, lying, pilfering and the like. For 1. These are the most dangerous faults, and therefore to be purged out more carefully. 2. In this way the child corrected will be the more clearly convicted of his fault, will condemn himself the more, and will bear the correction more contentedly.

With respect to the manner of correcting, four general and four #footnote[Four general rules for the manner of correcting.] particular rules are to be observed.

The general rules are these.

1\. An eye must be kept on God’s manner of correcting his children, and in particular on God’s correcting of the parent himself. No better general direction can be given, for God’s pattern is a perfect rule.

2\. Prayer must be made by parents for themselves and for their children: for themselves, to be directed in doing it; for their children, to be bettered by it. So good physicians will do in giving medicine. Prayer is to be used in all duties, and especially in this. For a parent is apt, partly through his own intemperate anger and partly through the child’s impatience, to fall into one extreme or the other. This is not to require of all that whenever they take up the rod they go and make a solemn prayer, but that they lift up the heart for direction and blessing.

3\. Correction must be given in love. “Do everything #footnote[1 Cor 16:14.] in love”; much more this, which carries an appearance of anger and hatred. In love parents will give medicine to their children, and splint a joint if need be. God corrects his children in love;#footnote[Rev 3:19.] so must parents. Love will make them do it with tenderness and compassion.

4\. Correction must be given in a mild mood, when the feelings #footnote[#emph[Nunquam, iratus qui accedit ad poenam, mediocritatem tenebit, Cic. Offic. lib.] 1. \[He who comes to punishment in anger will never keep to the middle way.\] Jer 30:11. Four particular rules for the manner of correcting.] are well ordered and not disordered by anger, rage, fury and other such passions. Disturbed passions cast a mist before the understanding, so that a man cannot discern what is enough and what is too much. When anger is stirred, correction must be put off. God corrects in #emph[measure.]

The particular rules are these.

1\. Due order must be kept. Correction by word must go before correction by the rod. “I rebuke and discipline,”#footnote[Rev 3:19. #emph[Vt ad vrendum & secandum medici, sic nos ad castigandum, rarò, inuitique veniamus, nec vnquam nisi necessario, si nulla reperietur alia medicina. Cic. Offic. lib.] 1. \[As physicians come to burning and cutting, so let us come to chastising seldom and unwillingly, and never except of necessity, if no other medicine can be found.\]] says the Lord. In this way a parent will show that he takes no delight in striking his child, but that it is necessity that forces him to it. In this way a parent shows himself like God, “who does not willingly afflict,” Lam 3:33. Physicians, when they give strong medicine, will give a preparative first; rebuke may serve as a preparative. Good and compassionate surgeons will try all other means before they come to lancing and searing.

2\. Due regard must be had to the one corrected. If he is young and tender, lighter correction must be used. Solomon often mentions a rod as most fitting for a child, for that is the lightest correction. So if the child is of a pliable and well-disposed nature, soon checked, the correction must be moderated accordingly. If he is well grown, and is also headstrong and stubborn, the correction may be more severe.

3\. Due regard must be had to the fault. Sins directly #footnote[Cavendum ne major poena, quàm culpa sit. Cic. loc. citat. \[Care must be taken that the punishment is not greater than the fault.\]] against God, open, notorious, scandalous sins, known sins, sins often committed, in which they have grown up and of which they have gotten a habit, are to be corrected with greater severity.

4\. In correcting his child’s faults, a parent must look at his own; so more compassion will be worked in him.

=== §. 48. #emph[Of parents’ too much indulgence.]

Contrary to this duty of correcting are two extremes.

+ Too much leniency.
+ Too much severity.

Many so pamper their children that they will allow them #footnote[#emph[Qui non corripiunt filios suos, & eorum animas igni inextinguibili in interitum tradunt, & semetipsos in furorem iudicij demergunt. Orig. in Iob. lib.] 1. \[Those who do not correct their sons both deliver their souls to destruction in unquenchable fire and plunge themselves into the fury of judgment.\] 1 Sam 2:29.] to run into any misbehavior rather than correct them. They cannot bear to hear their children cry, and therefore their children must be humored in all their whims and evil desires. These parents bring shame on themselves and harm on their children; for God is often forced to correct such children. God’s chastisements are heavy, and they often fall on parent and child both, as appears by the judgment on Eli and his sons. For such parents make themselves accessories to their children’s sins, and also to the judgments laid on their children. God says of such parents that they honor their children above God. It is therefore a foolish indulgence which makes parents regard neither God’s honor nor their own or their children’s safety.

Mothers for the most part offend in this. They are so far from performing this duty themselves that they are much offended with their husbands if the husbands do it. And to proclaim their folly to all the world, they cannot commit their children to a tutor or schoolmaster without a strict charge that he not correct them.

#emph[Objection.] Correction makes children dolts and blockheads.

#emph[Answer.] If God’s Spirit deserves more credence than such foolish women, that objection is utterly false; for we heard #footnote[§. 46.] before how correction gives wisdom and drives out foolishness.

2\. #emph[Objection.] Mothers may be borne with all the more in this, because they carried their children with long trouble and brought them forth with great labor.

#emph[Answer.] That may be a good motive for them to seek the true good of their children, so that their trouble and labor may not be in vain; but not to uphold their children in the broad way that leads to destruction.

=== §. 49. #emph[Of parents’ too much severity in correcting their children.]

Those who offend in the other extreme, of severity, are, of the #footnote[#emph[Patres non oportet asperos esse circa filios. Cyp. Testim. l.] 3. #emph[c.] 71. \[Fathers ought not to be harsh toward their sons.\]] two, the more heartless parents. They offend directly against the first branch of this text #emph[(Do not provoke to wrath).] Nothing provokes more than immoderate severity. For

1\. It shows no love in the parent, but rather hatred, or at least wrath and other such disordered feelings.

2\. It does not soften the child’s heart, but rather hardens it. #footnote[Verendum ne, si parentes ob quaevis errata filios coarguant, frontem tandem ipsi perfricent. Greg. Naz. ad Vital. \[It is to be feared that, if parents reprove their sons for every slip, the sons themselves will at last grow brazen.\]]

3\. It makes him dull and numb, and utterly perverts the right and true end of correction.

4\. It often makes a child think of doing some injury to his parent or to himself.

So that parents may take all the more heed of this extreme, I will briefly note in what ways excess in severity is shown.

It is shown when correction is given

1\. For no fault. In this case, however light the correction is, it is too severe.

2\. For small faults, in wrath and fury. Though the stripes are few and light, yet the parent’s behavior is too much severity.

3\. To children who are young and tender, or soft, mild and well-disposed, as if they were older and the most obstinate and perverse that could be.

4\. Too often, for everything done amiss; whereas parents #footnote[Multa quae vident parentes videre se dissimulent; multa se minime audisse fingant quae auribus infixa habent. Greg. Naz. ad Vital.] should seem not to see or hear many things which they do see and hear.

5\. Too hard, so that the child is lamed, or hurt in some way so that he will feel it as long as he lives.
